5. CHAIN OF KNOWLEDGE (CoK)
==================================================

The CoK is a persistent directed hypergraph
represented through a structured knowledge DSL.

OBJECT TYPES:

- CLAIM
- FACT
- RULE
- ASSUMPTION
- HYPOTHESIS
- DERIVATION
- EVIDENCE
- GOAL
- ACTION
- EXPERIMENT
- TEST_RESULT
- CONTRADICTION

RELATIONSHIP TYPES:

- REQUIRES
- IMPLIES
- SUPPORTS
- CONTRADICTS
- DEPENDS_ON
- PRODUCES
- TESTS
- ALTERNATIVE_TO

DERIVATION STRUCTURE:

TYPE: DERIVATION
ID: content_hash
CONCLUSION: claim_id
PREMISES: [claim_id, claim_id]
RULE: rule_id
ASSUMPTIONS: [...]
EVIDENCE: [...]
SOURCE_REFERENCES: [...]
VERIFICATION: status
SCHEMA_VERSION: version

KNOWLEDGE STATES:

PROVISIONAL
VERIFIED
DISPUTED
INVALIDATED
UNRESOLVED
ARCHIVED

Every derivation preserves:
- Premises
- Applied inference rule
- Assumptions
- Supporting evidence
- Source provenance
- Verification status
- Dependency references

Formal logical validity and empirical truth
are tracked independently.

Contradictions and invalidated derivations
remain traceable.


==================================================
6. KNOWLEDGE DSL
==================================================

GammaThink uses a deterministic,
machine-parsable Domain-Specific Language
as its canonical logical representation.

The knowledge base is not primarily
a collection of natural-language paragraphs.

Instead, it contains structured objects
describing claims, entities, rules,
relationships, evidence, and derivations.

The DSL is shared between:

- Neural language interpreter
- Knowledge compiler
- Symbolic reasoning engine
- Verification engine
- Snapshot compiler
- Human graph visualiser

The DSL describes logical knowledge
independently of physical storage.

GKS snapshots may store a compact binary
encoding of the same logical structure.

Human-readable DSL can be reconstructed
from the binary representation when needed.

The reasoning engine works with parsed
knowledge objects rather than repeatedly
interpreting natural-language descriptions.


==================================================
7. EXAMPLE KNOWLEDGE DSL
==================================================

CLAIM C001 {
    SUBJECT: "water"
    PROPERTY: "freezing_point"
    VALUE: 0
    UNIT: "celsius"
    CONDITIONS: {
        pressure: "1_atm"
        substance: "pure_water"
    }
    STATUS: VERIFIED
    EVIDENCE: [E001]
}

CLAIM C002 {
    SUBJECT: "sample_A"
    PROPERTY: "temperature"
    VALUE: -5
    UNIT: "celsius"
    STATUS: VERIFIED
    EVIDENCE: [E002]
}

RULE R001 {
    NAME: "freezing_condition"
    PREMISES: [C001, C002]
    ADDITIONAL_CONDITIONS: [
        "sample_A_is_pure_water",
        "sample_A_pressure_is_1_atm",
        "equilibrium_conditions_apply"
    ]
    CONCLUSION: C003
}

CLAIM C003 {
    SUBJECT: "sample_A"
    PROPERTY: "equilibrium_phase"
    VALUE: "solid"
    STATUS: PROVISIONAL
}

DERIVATION D001 {
    PREMISES: [C001, C002]
    RULE: R001
    CONCLUSION: C003
    ASSUMPTIONS: [
        "sample_A_is_pure_water",
        "sample_A_pressure_is_1_atm",
        "equilibrium_conditions_apply"
    ]
    VERIFICATION: PENDING
}

This is illustrative DSL syntax.

The final implementation requires a
versioned grammar and deterministic parser.

Empirical claims must distinguish measured
facts, assumptions, and conditional rules.


==================================================
8. HYPERGRAPH REPRESENTATION
==================================================

The parsed DSL forms a directed,
typed knowledge hypergraph.

A hypergraph can represent relationships
involving multiple premises simultaneously.

EXAMPLE:

       +-----------+       +-----------+
       | CLAIM C001|       | CLAIM C002|
       +-----+-----+       +-----+-----+
             |                   |
             +---------+---------+
                       |
                       v
                +-------------+
                | RULE R001   |
                | DERIVATION  |
                | D001        |
                +------+------+
                       |
                       v
                +-------------+
                | CLAIM C003  |
                +-------------+

The derivation requires the combined
premises and applicable conditions.

It is not equivalent to independent
binary implications from each premise.

SUPPORTED STRUCTURES:

- Multi-premise inference
- Alternative derivations
- Shared dependencies
- Cross-domain relationships
- Contradictory hypotheses
- Evidence-to-claim relationships
- Provenance tracing
- Recursive dependency traversal
- Multiple conclusions where applicable

Each derivation is represented by an
explicit relationship object or hyperedge.


